INT. ABANDONED CONSERVATORY - GLASSHOUSE - DAY

Dead palms. Cracked panes. A wrought-iron table in the center, set for tea. INSPECTOR CLEMENTINE PRYCE (50s, tailored suit, kid gloves) pours from a silver pot. Across from her sits SIR ALDOUS WREN (70s, cravat, a cane with a silver head), perfectly composed.

                    CLEMENTINE
          Sugar, Sir Aldous?

                    ALDOUS
          Two, Inspector. As always.

She drops two cubes into his cup. He stirs, lifts it, and smiles.

                    ALDOUS (CONT'D)
          You've grown quiet, my dear Clementine.

                    CLEMENTINE
          I'm listening to the glass. It still sings in the wind.

                    ALDOUS
          It sang the night my brother died.

She sets down the pot very gently. Her gloved hand rests on the table.

                    CLEMENTINE
          You were here that night, Aldous.

                    ALDOUS
          I was in London.

                    CLEMENTINE
          (kindly)
          Your cuff, Sir Aldous. There's a leaf on it.

He glances down. A single pale frond, the kind that grows only in this room, clings to the cloth. His smile does not move, but his fingers tighten on the cup.

                    ALDOUS
          Clementine, my dear. You were always the clever one.

                    CLEMENTINE
          And you, Aldous, were always the early riser.

She raises a hand. Beyond the glass, two constables step from the hedges, and he sets the cup down without a sound.
